        "text": "Put the four pillars together and the rule in the title stops sounding aspirational. Every AI change (a prompt tweak, a model bump, a retrieval config, an agent-authored PR) opens with an isolated deployment, a copy-on-write data branch with fresh credentials, and an eval run whose thresholds are a required check. The reviewer gets one link, watches the behavior on production-shaped data, and reads a per-rubric score before approving. Lock previews behind deployment protection when the content is sensitive, lean on the default `noindex` so nothing leaks to search, and treat tear-down as mandatory: an un-reaped Vercel deployment or an idle Neon branch is a slow bill, not a spare."
